WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.090
Our corridor is dark by five, and

2
00:00:03.090 --> 00:00:04.850
someone always forgets the light.

3
00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:07.640
My pupils built a light that

4
00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:08.940
switches itself on.

5
00:00:09.670 --> 00:00:12.970
A light sensor, an LED and a tiny

6
00:00:12.970 --> 00:00:15.150
computer, smaller than a bank card.

7
00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:16.690
Let me show you what's inside.

8
00:00:18.030 --> 00:00:21.030
Today: microcontrollers, the tiny

9
00:00:21.030 --> 00:00:24.250
computers that sense, decide and act.

10
00:00:25.470 --> 00:00:28.090
A microcontroller is a whole small

11
00:00:28.090 --> 00:00:30.790
computer on one chip: a processor,

12
00:00:31.350 --> 00:00:33.730
memory, and pins that connect to the

13
00:00:33.730 --> 00:00:33.950
world.

14
00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:37.080
It's inside washing machines, phone

15
00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:39.840
chargers, prepaid meters and weather

16
00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:40.200
stations.

17
00:00:41.020 --> 00:00:44.080
Boards like the Arduino, the micro:bit and

18
00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:47.660
the ESP32 make them easy to use in class.

19
00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:51.360
Its pins are inputs and outputs.

20
00:00:52.180 --> 00:00:55.560
Inputs bring news in: a button, a

21
00:00:55.560 --> 00:00:57.240
light sensor, a thermometer.

22
00:00:57.870 --> 00:01:01.370
Outputs act: an LED, a buzzer, or

23
00:01:01.370 --> 00:01:02.570
a motor through a driver.

24
00:01:03.220 --> 00:01:05.080
Analog input pins turn a

25
00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:06.520
voltage into a number.

26
00:01:07.770 --> 00:01:09.350
The program has two parts.

27
00:01:10.150 --> 00:01:12.850
Setup runs once, when the power comes on.

28
00:01:13.460 --> 00:01:16.920
Then the loop runs again and again, many

29
00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:22.140
times a second: read, decide, act, wait.

30
00:01:22.770 --> 00:01:24.910
That loop is the heart of

31
00:01:24.910 --> 00:01:26.050
every smart device.

32
00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:28.560
Here's the night light.

33
00:01:29.260 --> 00:01:31.640
Read the light sensor: more light

34
00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:34.020
gives a bigger number, from zero to

35
00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:35.500
one thousand and twenty-three.

36
00:01:36.070 --> 00:01:38.750
Decide: below three hundred means dark.

37
00:01:39.410 --> 00:01:43.310
Act: switch the LED on, or else off.

38
00:01:44.010 --> 00:01:45.350
Wait a tenth of a second,

39
00:01:45.870 --> 00:01:46.730
and go round again.

40
00:01:47.990 --> 00:01:48.390
Quick check!

41
00:01:49.010 --> 00:01:51.770
The sensor reads eight hundred, and our

42
00:01:51.770 --> 00:01:54.450
rule says dark is below three hundred.

43
00:01:55.110 --> 00:01:57.130
Is the LED on or off?

44
00:01:58.420 --> 00:01:58.760
Off.

45
00:01:59.450 --> 00:02:01.390
Eight hundred is well above three

46
00:02:01.390 --> 00:02:02.790
hundred, so it's bright.

47
00:02:03.460 --> 00:02:05.540
The loop will check again in

48
00:02:05.540 --> 00:02:06.600
a tenth of a second.

49
00:02:07.870 --> 00:02:09.510
There are three ways to start.

50
00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:12.480
With a board: an Arduino or a

51
00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:15.360
micro:bit, a few LEDs and a light sensor.

52
00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:18.280
With a phone or a computer: free

53
00:02:18.280 --> 00:02:20.380
simulators like MakeCode and

54
00:02:20.380 --> 00:02:22.040
Wokwi run in a browser.

55
00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:24.680
And with nothing at all: one

56
00:02:24.680 --> 00:02:26.440
pupil plays the microcontroller

57
00:02:26.440 --> 00:02:28.000
and follows a program card.

58
00:02:29.230 --> 00:02:30.810
In the lab, you'll see how a

59
00:02:30.810 --> 00:02:33.670
microcontroller turns a smooth signal into

60
00:02:33.670 --> 00:02:34.010
numbers.

61
00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:37.280
Choose how often it measures, and how many

62
00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:39.860
bits it uses, and watch what's lost.

63
00:02:41.220 --> 00:02:41.760
Your turn.

64
00:02:42.370 --> 00:02:45.870
On cloudy days our corridor is dim, and

65
00:02:45.870 --> 00:02:47.690
the night light switches on at noon.

66
00:02:48.350 --> 00:02:49.410
What should we change?

67
00:02:50.850 --> 00:02:53.270
Lower the number in the rule, say to

68
00:02:53.270 --> 00:02:55.730
two hundred: then it only counts as

69
00:02:55.730 --> 00:02:57.490
dark when it's really dark.

70
00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:00.620
Remember: a microcontroller reads

71
00:03:00.620 --> 00:03:02.740
its inputs, decides with a rule,

72
00:03:03.240 --> 00:03:04.980
acts on its outputs, and loops.

73
00:03:05.720 --> 00:03:07.660
Change the rule, and you

74
00:03:07.660 --> 00:03:08.420
change the device.

75
00:03:09.850 --> 00:03:12.370
Try it in class tomorrow: one

76
00:03:12.370 --> 00:03:14.510
pupil is the microcontroller.

77
00:03:15.070 --> 00:03:17.530
Give her a program card: look at the

78
00:03:17.530 --> 00:03:20.450
window, decide with the rule, and

79
00:03:20.450 --> 00:03:22.210
switch a torch on or off.

80
00:03:22.770 --> 00:03:25.330
Then, if you can, build the same

81
00:03:25.330 --> 00:03:26.610
night light in a simulator.
